{
  "name": "How to Prepare for a Roof Damage Assessment in Anna",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1: Document the Event Date — Make a note of when the storm or damage occurred. This helps establish a timeline, which is often crucial for insurance claims and understanding the cause of the damage. Accurate dating provides a solid reference point for the assessment team.",
      "text": "Step 1: Document the Event Date — Make a note of when the storm or damage occurred. This helps establish a timeline, which is often crucial for insurance claims and understanding the cause of the damage. Accurate dating provides a solid reference point for the assessment team.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 2: Take Initial Photos (Safely!) — If it's safe to do so, take some initial photos from the ground. Capture overall roof planes, any obvious damage, and surrounding areas. This gives a preliminary visual record and helps establish the condition immediately after the event, but never put yourself at risk.",
      "text": "Step 2: Take Initial Photos (Safely!) — If it's safe to do so, take some initial photos from the ground. Capture overall roof planes, any obvious damage, and surrounding areas. This gives a preliminary visual record and helps establish the condition immediately after the event, but never put yourself at risk.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 2
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 3: Preserve Records — Gather any relevant maintenance records, prior inspection reports, or warranty information for your roof. These documents provide valuable context about your roof's history and can help the assessor understand its pre-storm condition and age.",
      "text": "Step 3: Preserve Records — Gather any relevant maintenance records, prior inspection reports, or warranty information for your roof. These documents provide valuable context about your roof's history and can help the assessor understand its pre-storm condition and age.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 3
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 4: Clear Access to Your Property — Ensure the assessment team has clear and safe access to your property and the roof, if possible. Remove any obstacles around the house and make sure pets are secured. Clear access allows the team to thoroughly inspect all necessary areas without hindrance.",
      "text": "Step 4: Clear Access to Your Property — Ensure the assessment team has clear and safe access to your property and the roof, if possible. Remove any obstacles around the house and make sure pets are secured. Clear access allows the team to thoroughly inspect all necessary areas without hindrance.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 4
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 5: Review Your Insurance Policy — Take a moment to review your homeowner's insurance policy. Understand your coverage, deductibles, and any specific requirements for reporting damage. This knowledge empowers you during the assessment and any subsequent insurance claim process.",
      "text": "Preparing your home and having your information ready helps us provide the most accurate and helpful damage assessment. It's important to act promptly after damage occurs to prevent further issues and ensure proper documentation. Don't make permanent repairs or discard damaged materials before the assessment, except for reasonable emergency measures to prevent further damage; just photograph everything first. Ready to get your roof assessed the right way? Contact Discounted Roofing Co. for expert guidance and support.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 5
    }
  ],
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "speakable": {
    "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
    "cssSelector": [
      "h1",
      ".howto-step",
      "[itemprop=\"step\"]"
    ]
  },
